🛡️ Defense
	1. Redis authentication enabled.
	2. Bind Redis to localhost only.
	3. No gopher/file schemes allowed.
	4. Strict outbound firewall.

🔥 15️. SSR→ Internal OAuth Service → Token Theftft
Very senior-level chain.

🏗️ Architecture
User → App
App talks to internal OAuth server:
Example:
http://auth.internal.local/token
Used for service-to-service authentication.

🧠 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Your app normally calls auth server using internal credentials. App has SSRF in URL preview feature.
Step 2 – Attacker forces app to call:
http://auth.internal.local/token?client_id=app&grant_type=client_credentials
Step 3 – App automatically attaches its own internal credentials.
Step 4 – OAuth server returns access token.
Step 5 – Attacker extracts token from response.
Step 6 – Use token to call internal APIs.
Full lateral movement.

🔥 Why This Is Dangerous
Internal auth systems assume:
“Caller is trusted service.”
SSRF breaks identity boundary.

🛡️ Defense
	1. Require mutual TLS.
	2. Bind tokens to audience + IP.
	3. Do not auto-attach credentials blindly.
	4. Strict outbound restrictions.

🧠 The Deep Pattern Across All 5
-> There exists something accessible only internally.
-> That thing trusts internal callers.
-> SSRF makes your app act as internal caller.
-> Attacker benefits from that trust.

SSRF is a trust boundary violation where untrusted input controls server-side network identity, enabling lateral movement, credential extraction, or privilege escalation inside otherwise isolated systems.

